# The area map — machine-readable. THE source; there is no second copy.
#
# WHY IT LIVES IN THE BACKEND. It partitions the backend, and this repo's CI must read it: ci/
# check_round.sh computes which areas a PR touches from its diff and requires the PR body to declare
# them. A map in the workspace repo could not be read by a runner that only checks out this one, so
# the round declaration could never be a gate — and prose loses to green CI.
#
# The prose that explains it — why areas are not seams, area != module, concepts that cross areas,
# and who reviews the surfaces that have no areas — is the workspace registry,
# ../../../docs/00_workspace/areas.md. That file owns the reasoning; this file owns the data.
# `harness/check.sh areas` parses THIS file, so the two cannot drift.
#
# Format: an unindented line opens an area; indented lines are globs relative to this repo's root.
# `!unowned` lists paths that genuinely belong to no area, each with a reason, so an exclusion is a
# visible statement rather than a hole. A path matching no glob FAILS the gate, on purpose: the
# routing map this replaces covered 231 of 322 files and failed by being quiet.

